========================================================================
PLAIN ENGLISH (RULES 1.10)
========================================================================

**What changed**
`aes_encrypt` in `ooda/std/core/crypto.oo` now calls `process_exit(1)`
before any string return. It is **not** encryption. A dead
`"STUB_FAIL_CLOSED"` return keeps the String type. SHA-256 / HMAC
bodies are unchanged. C `crypto_aes_encrypt_internal` is unchanged.

**Why it matters**
The old wrapper returned the C stub string. That looks like ciphertext
(SINS #3 Pride / LORE #4). Callers of `aes_encrypt("k","p")` now die
fail-closed (exit 1). They cannot treat a residual string as AES.

**What is not done**
- C AES-128-ECB + `STUB_FAIL_CLOSED` still live in `chs_rt_hash.c`
  (M162; smokes call the C name, not this wrapper).
- SHA-256 verify is still length-only (F41). NIST `hello` mismatch
  in C is pre-existing; not this OWN.
- SPRINT F40 row not edited (forbidden).

**What to do next**
Docs may mark F40 closed. Do not flip C residual to `exit(1)` without
rewriting `crypto_md5_sha1_smoke.oo` + `m162_residual_deepen_smoke.oo`.

