### Prove 1 — tiny call `sha256("hello")` still works

`oodac check ooda/std/core/crypto.oo` → `OK`

`HOME=/home/jeryd bash ooda/scripts/ooda_test_verify.oo ooda/std/core/crypto.oo`

```
OKtestharness 1 asserts from 1 verify
OK verify (1 asserts)
```

That harness runs `assert_eq!(sha256("hello").len(), 64)`.

Stripped-copy + `main { println(sha256("hello")); }` emit-c + gcc:

```
sha_out=ceb3257a5faba90e26e83b2ac5b9e29ef8ac2be41fa2485e73043362938b9824
len=64 rc=0
```

Same 64 hex via free-name `crypto_sha256_internal("hello")` (C
untouched). NIST `hello` is `2cf24dba…`; C mismatch is **pre-existing**
(F41 residual). Not rewritten.

### Prove 2 — `aes_encrypt("k","p")` is not success-with-ciphertext

Stripped-copy + `main { println(aes_encrypt("k","p")); }` emit-c + gcc:

```
aes_out=[] aes_rc=1
```

Emit body: `oo_process_exit(1LL);` then dead `oo_str_lit("STUB_FAIL_CLOSED")`.

16-byte also `aes16_rc=1` empty out (Path B had printed hex).

### C regression (not a prove of F40, honesty)

`bash ooda/scripts/crypto_md5_sha1_smoke.oo` → `PASSED`
(C AES residual string still present; MD5/SHA1 vectors OK).

